# Lesson 2: DATA Step and PROC Step

## Objective

This lesson introduces the basic difference between the SAS DATA step and PROC step.

The DATA step is used to create, read, modify, and prepare SAS datasets.

The PROC step is used to display, analyse, summarise, and report data.

## Concepts Covered

This lesson covers creating a SAS dataset using the DATA step.

It also covers reading an existing dataset using the SET statement.

The lesson uses SAS-provided sample datasets.

The lesson uses PROC PRINT to display data.

The lesson also includes SAS log review and validation checks.

## Practice 1: Creating a Student Dataset

The following program creates a new dataset named student_copy by reading the SAS-provided SASHELP.CLASS dataset.

data student_copy;
    set sashelp.class;
run;

proc print data=student_copy;
run;

## Explanation

The DATA statement starts the DATA step and creates the student_copy dataset.

The SET statement reads observations from the SAS-provided SASHELP.CLASS dataset.

The RUN statement executes the DATA step.

The PROC PRINT statement displays the observations in the student_copy dataset.

## Independent Exercise: Creating a Vehicle Dataset

The following program creates a new dataset named vehicle_review by reading the SAS-provided SASHELP.CARS dataset.

data vehicle_review;
    set sashelp.cars;
run;

proc print data=vehicle_review;
run;

## Explanation

The DATA statement starts the DATA step and creates the vehicle_review dataset.

The SET statement reads observations from the SAS-provided SASHELP.CARS dataset.

The RUN statement executes the DATA step.

The PROC PRINT statement displays the observations in the vehicle_review dataset.

## Difference Between DATA Step and PROC Step

The DATA step is mainly used to read, create, modify, and prepare datasets.

The PROC step is mainly used to display, analyse, summarise, and report data.

